[644076,"messestand","noun","stall",1,"m"]]},"nacht der langen messer":{"entries":[["nacht der langen messer",null,"/ˈnaxt der ˌlaŋən ˈmɛsər/","Literally, “night of the long knives”. The term had been in use since at least 1932 for a murderous reckoning that the Nazis were expected to conduct against their political enemies in the first night(s) after a potential rise to power. Such a single outburst did not happen, although many people were killed or sent to concentration camps over the following months. When the purge took place in 1934, the expression was transferred to this event (which was of course of rather a different nature).",{"w":"Nacht der langen Messer","g":"f"}]],"senses":[[650034,"nacht der langen messer","noun","night of the long knives (purge in which opponents of a regime or political party are killed or removed)",0,"f"],[650035,"nacht der langen messer","noun","synonym of Röhm-Putsch (“1934 purge in Nazi Germany called the Night of the Long Knives”)",1,"f",["archaic","Nazism"]]]},"nichtsdestotrotz":{"entries":[["nichtsdestotrotz",null,"/ˌnɪçtsdɛstoˈtʁɔts/","Blend of nichtsdestoweniger + trotzdem. Originally humorous and colloquial, but penetrated into the standard register by the second half of the 20th century. Already in 1958, a textbook remarks on existing usage in a serious context. In the DWDS newspaper corpus, it overtook the earlier standard nichtsdestoweniger in usage around 1990, and has become the dominant term since. By surface analysis, nichts + desto + trotz."]],"senses":[[36582,"nichtsdestotrotz","adv","nonetheless",0]]},"nützlicheren":{"entries":[["nützlicheren",null,null,null]],"senses":[[154481,"nützlicheren","adj","inflection of nützlich:",0],[154482,"nützlicheren","adj","strong genitive masculine/neuter singular comparative degree",1],[154483,"nützlicheren","adj","weak/mixed genitive/dative all-gender singular comparative degree",2],[154484,"nützlicheren","adj","strong/weak/mixed accusative masculine singular comparative degree"